= Solution
{parent=a}

For a <holomorphic function> with $z=re^{i\theta}$, the <chain rule> gives
$$
f_r=e^{i\theta}f'(z),\qquad f_\theta=ire^{i\theta}f'(z)=irf_r.
$$
Writing $f=u+iv$ and equating <real parts> and <imaginary parts>,
$$
u_\theta=-rv_r,\qquad v_\theta=ru_r.
$$
For $r>0$, division by $r$ gives the <Cauchy-Riemann equations> in <polar coordinates>:
$$
\boxed{\frac1r u_\theta=-v_r,\qquad u_r=\frac1r v_\theta.}
$$
The restriction $r>0$ reflects the coordinate singularity at the origin; the Cartesian equations have no such restriction.

= b
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Insert the specified real part into the polar <Cauchy-Riemann equations>. The first gives $v_r=nr^{n-1}\sin(n\theta)$, and integration with respect to $r$ gives
$$
v(r,\theta)=r^n\sin(n\theta)+C(\theta).
$$
The second equation then requires $v_\theta=nr^n\cos(n\theta)$, so $C'(\theta)=0$. The remaining imaginary additive constant is fixed by $f(0)=0$. Thus
$$
\boxed{v(r,\theta)=r^n\sin(n\theta),\qquad f(z)=r^ne^{in\theta}=z^n.}
$$
The resulting <polynomial> is holomorphic at the origin too, consistently extending the calculation made in polar coordinates with $r>0$.

= c
{parent=7}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Using the exponential form of the complex cosine, $\cos z=0$ is equivalent to $e^{2iz}=-1$. Thus the <zeros> are exactly
$$
\boxed{z_n=\frac\pi2+n\pi,\qquad n\in\mathbb Z.}
$$
At each point $\cos'(z_n)=-\sin z_n\ne0$, so the cosine has a simple zero. Cubing it gives
$$
\cos^3z=[-\sin z_n]^3(z-z_n)^3+O((z-z_n)^4).
$$
The <order of a zero of a holomorphic function> is therefore three at every zero. There are no finite singularities because this is an <entire function>.

To classify infinity, examine $\cos^3(1/w)$ at $w=0$. The identity $\cos^3z=(3\cos z+\cos3z)/4$ shows that its <Laurent series> contains the nonzero negative-power coefficients
$$
\frac{(-1)^m(3+3^{2m})}{4(2m)!}\,w^{-2m},\qquad m\geq1.
$$
There are infinitely many terms in the <principal part>, the criterion for an <essential singularity>. Therefore $\boxed{\infty\text{ is an essential singularity, not a pole.}}$ This is also the general characterization of a nonpolynomial <entire function> at infinity.

= d
{parent=7}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

If $z_0\ne0$, factor out $z_0$ and use the <geometric series>:
$$
\frac1{z_0+z}=\frac1{z_0}\frac1{1+z/z_0}=\sum_{n=0}^\infty\frac{(-1)^n}{z_0^{n+1}}z^n,\qquad |z|<|z_0|.
$$
Thus
$$
\boxed{a_n=\frac{(-1)^n}{z_0^{n+1}},\qquad R=|z_0|.}
$$
The <radius of convergence> is the distance to the nearest singularity, the <simple pole> at $z=-z_0$. The case $z_0=0$ must be treated separately: the function is $1/z$, has no <Taylor series> about zero and is already its own <Laurent series> on $0<|z|<\infty$.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

For $|z|<1$, multiply the absolutely convergent <Taylor series> of the sine by the <geometric series>:
$$
\frac{\sin z}{1-z^2}=\left[\sum_{j=0}^\infty\frac{(-1)^jz^{2j+1}}{(2j+1)!}\right]\left[\sum_{k=0}^\infty z^{2k}\right].
$$
The product is odd, so all even coefficients vanish. Collecting the coefficient of $z^{2m+1}$ gives
$$
\boxed{a_{2m}=0,\qquad a_{2m+1}=\sum_{j=0}^m\frac{(-1)^j}{(2j+1)!},\qquad m=0,1,2,\ldots.}
$$
For example, the first terms are $z+5z^3/6+101z^5/120+\cdots$. The denominator vanishes at $z=\pm1$, and the numerator is nonzero there, so these are genuine <simple poles>. Therefore $\boxed{R=1}$ is the <radius of convergence>.

= 8
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

After division by the leading coefficient, write $y''+P(x)y'+Q(x)y=0$, where
$$
P(x)=-\frac1{x(1+x)},\qquad Q(x)=\frac{x+3}{4x^2(1+x)}.
$$
The only finite <singular points of a differential equation> are $\boxed{x=0\text{ and }x=-1}$. At zero, $xP=-1/(1+x)$ and $x^2Q=(x+3)/[4(1+x)]$ are analytic. At $-1$, $(x+1)P=-1/x$ and $(x+1)^2Q=(x+1)(x+3)/(4x^2)$ are analytic. The <regular singular point criterion for a second-order equation> therefore makes both points regular.

If infinity is included, it is a <regular singular point at infinity> as well: $P(x)=O(x^{-2})$ and $Q(x)=1/(4x^2)+O(x^{-3})$. Equivalently, with $t=1/x$ the transformed coefficients are $\widetilde P=2/t+1/(1+t)$ and $\widetilde Q=(1+3t)/[4t^2(1+t)]$, which satisfy the same regular-singular criterion at $t=0$.

= b
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

For the <Frobenius solution>, differentiate term by term:
$$
y'=\sum_{n\geq0}(n+\sigma)a_nx^{n+\sigma-1},\qquad y''=\sum_{n\geq0}(n+\sigma)(n+\sigma-1)a_nx^{n+\sigma-2}.
$$
The unshifted terms in the equation have coefficient $[4(n+\sigma)(n+\sigma-1)-4(n+\sigma)+3]a_n$. The extra factors of $x$ give the shifted contribution $[4(n+\sigma)(n+\sigma-1)+1]a_nx^{n+\sigma+1}$. Equating the coefficient of $x^{k+\sigma}$ for $k\geq1$ gives the <linear recurrence relation>
$$
\boxed{[4(k+\sigma)^2-8(k+\sigma)+3]a_k+[4(k+\sigma-1)(k+\sigma-2)+1]a_{k-1}=0.}
$$
Factoring both coefficients is especially useful:
$$
\boxed{(2k+2\sigma-3)\big[(2k+2\sigma-1)a_k+(2k+2\sigma-3)a_{k-1}\big]=0.}
$$
The separate lowest-power equation is $(4\sigma^2-8\sigma+3)a_0=0$. Do not cancel $2k+2\sigma-3$ when it vanishes: that is the possible <undetermined coefficient at Frobenius resonance>.

= c
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Because $a_0\ne0$, the lowest-power equation from part (b) requires the <indicial equation>
$$
4\sigma^2-8\sigma+3=(2\sigma-1)(2\sigma-3)=0.
$$
The two candidate <indicial roots> are therefore
$$
\boxed{\sigma=\frac12\quad\text{and}\quad\sigma=\frac32.}
$$
Their difference is the integer one, so the smaller-root coefficient equation at $k=1$ needs examination rather than automatic division.

= d
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=d}

For the larger <indicial root> $\sigma=3/2$, the recurrence in part (b) becomes
$$
2k[(2k+2)a_k+2ka_{k-1}]=0,\qquad k\geq1.
$$
No factor being cancelled is zero in this range, so
$$
\boxed{a_k=-\frac{k}{k+1}a_{k-1}=\frac{(-1)^k}{k+1}a_0.}
$$
Thus the resulting <Frobenius solution> is
$$
\boxed{y(x)=a_0x^{3/2}\sum_{k=0}^\infty\frac{(-x)^k}{k+1}=a_0x^{3/2}\left(1-\frac x2+\frac{x^2}3-\cdots\right).}
$$
There is exactly **one free parameter**, $a_0$, setting the overall scale. The analytic series factor has <radius of convergence> one. For a real-valued expression take $x>0$; a complex version requires a local square-root branch.

= e
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=e}

The <Taylor series> of the logarithm gives
$$
\log(1+x)=\sum_{j=1}^\infty\frac{(-1)^{j+1}}j x^j=x\sum_{k=0}^\infty\frac{(-x)^k}{k+1}.
$$
Therefore the closed form of the larger-root <Frobenius solution> is
$$
\boxed{y(x)=a_0\sqrt{x}\log(1+x).}
$$
The series derivation holds initially for $0<x<1$; the closed form extends as a real solution to all $x>0$. To check it without series, put $y=\sqrt{x}\,v(x)$ in the original equation. All undifferentiated $v$ terms cancel and the remaining expression is $4x^{5/2}[(1+x)v''+v']$. Hence $[(1+x)v']'=0$, and $v=\log(1+x)$ indeed solves it. Its logarithm is analytic near zero, so this is not a forced $\log x$ companion term at the Frobenius point.

= f
{parent=8}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=f}

At the smaller <indicial root> $\sigma=1/2$, the $k=1$ equation is $0=0$: it leaves $a_1$ free. For $k\geq2$, the valid recurrence is
$$
a_k=-\frac{k-1}{k}a_{k-1}.
$$
Choosing $a_1=0$ makes every later coefficient zero. The resulting <terminating Frobenius series> is therefore
$$
\boxed{y(x)=a_0\sqrt{x}.}
$$
Substitution gives $y'=a_0/(2\sqrt{x})$ and $y''=-a_0/(4x^{3/2})$; the three terms in the equation sum to $a_0\sqrt{x}[-(1+x)-2+(x+3)]=0$.

Termination is a permitted choice, not a condition forced on every smaller-root solution. Retaining the free $a_1$ adds the solution in part (e), so the general real solution on $x>0$ is $\boxed{y=\sqrt{x}[C_1+C_2\log(1+x)]}$. This is exactly the freedom explained by <undetermined coefficient at Frobenius resonance>. On either negative interval avoiding $-1$ and $0$, analogous real formulas use $\sqrt{|x|}$ and $\log|1+x|$.

= 9
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

For fixed endpoint values, a stationary differentiable path obeys the <Euler-Lagrange equation>
$$
\boxed{\frac{\partial f}{\partial y}-\frac d{dx}\left(\frac{\partial f}{\partial y'}\right)=0.}
$$
It follows by taking the <first variation> of the functional and integrating the term in $\delta y'$ by parts; $\delta y$ vanishes at the endpoints.

= b
{parent=9}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

If $f$ has no explicit $x$ dependence, the <chain rule> gives
$$
\frac{df}{dx}=f_y y'+f_{y'}y''.
$$
Consequently
$$
\frac d{dx}(f-y'f_{y'})=y'\left(f_y-\frac d{dx}f_{y'}\right)=0
$$
on an <Euler-Lagrange equation> solution. Thus the <Beltrami identity> supplies the <first integral>
$$
\boxed{h=f-y'\frac{\partial f}{\partial y'}=\text{constant}.}
$$
The opposite sign is an equally valid convention. The conserved value can be zero for particular integrands or paths; absence of explicit $x$ alone does not guarantee a nonzero numerical value. The useful statement is that this expression is constant along an extremal.

= c
{parent=9}
{scope}

= i
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

On the cylindrical surface $r=R$, the <line element> is $ds^2=R^2d\theta^2+dz^2$. For a single-valued path parametrized by increasing $\theta$,
$$
\boxed{L[z]=\int_{\theta_1}^{\theta_2}\sqrt{R^2+z'^2}\,d\theta.}
$$
The integrand has no explicit $z$, so its <Euler-Lagrange equation> gives the constant conjugate quantity $z'/\sqrt{R^2+z'^2}$. Its <derivative> with respect to $z'$ is strictly positive, hence $z'$ itself is constant. The two endpoints determine
$$
\boxed{z(\theta)=z_1+\frac{z_2-z_1}{\pi}\left(\theta+\frac\pi2\right),\qquad-\frac\pi2\leq\theta\leq\frac\pi2.}
$$
This is a constant-pitch <helix> on the cylinder. To verify global minimality, unroll the cylinder with planar coordinates $(R\theta,z)$. The path becomes a straight line between points separated by $\pi R$ and $z_2-z_1$, so the <triangle inequality> for arc length gives
$$
\boxed{L_{\min}=\sqrt{\pi^2R^2+(z_2-z_1)^2}.}
$$
This is the standard description of <geodesics on a circular cylinder>.

= ii
{parent=c}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The downward drop from the starting rim to angle $\theta$ is $R\cos\theta$. With no dissipative forces, <conservation of energy> gives $mv^2/2=mgR\cos\theta$, so $v=\sqrt{2gR\cos\theta}$. Dividing the <line element> by this speed yields
$$
\boxed{T[z]=\frac1{\sqrt{2gR}}\int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2}\sqrt{\frac{R^2+z'^2}{\cos\theta}}\,d\theta.}
$$
The endpoint singularities are integrable for the minimizing path. The integrand is independent of $z$; its <Euler-Lagrange equation> gives a constant $K$, after absorbing the overall prefactor:
$$
\frac{z'}{\sqrt{\cos\theta}\sqrt{R^2+z'^2}}=K.
$$
Solving for $z'$ and taking the positive branch required by $z_2>z_1$ gives
$$
\boxed{\frac{dz}{d\theta}=\frac{RK\sqrt{\cos\theta}}{\sqrt{1-K^2\cos\theta}},\qquad0<K<1.}
$$
The constant is fixed implicitly by
$$
z_2-z_1=RK\int_{-\pi/2}^{\pi/2}\frac{\sqrt{\cos\theta}}{\sqrt{1-K^2\cos\theta}}\,d\theta.
$$
This integral is strictly increasing from zero to infinity as $K$ increases from zero to one, so every finite positive separation selects a unique $K$. The time integrand has second <derivative> $R^2/[\sqrt{\cos\theta}(R^2+z'^2)^{3/2}]>0$ with respect to $z'$. Its supporting-line inequality, integrated between fixed endpoints with constant first <derivative> with respect to $z'$, proves this stationary path is the unique <global minimum> within the stated single-valued class.

As $\theta\downarrow-\pi/2$, $z'\to0$. Therefore the skater should initially head **directly down the curved ramp, perpendicular to the cylinder axis**, and gain axial motion later. The path differs from the constant-pitch shortest route because extra distance is cheaper in time near the faster, lower part of the ramp. This is the <brachistochrone on a horizontal half-cylinder>.

\Image[paper-1-half-cylinder-paths.png]
{title=Shortest and fastest routes on the unrolled half-cylinder for $z_2-z_1=2R$}

= 10
{parent=Paper 1}
{scope}

= a
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=a}

Expand the two operators before comparing coefficients:
$$
\widetilde L y=-\widetilde a\,y''-(\widetilde a'+\widetilde b)y'-\widetilde c\,y,\qquad Ly=-aw\,y''-(aw)'y'-wc\,y.
$$
Equality $L=w\widetilde L$ requires $a=\widetilde a$, $c=\widetilde c$, and $(aw)'=w(a'+\widetilde b)$. Thus $aw'=\widetilde b w$, giving the positive <integrating factor>
$$
\boxed{a=\widetilde a,\qquad c=\widetilde c,\qquad w(x)=C\exp\left[\int_{x_0}^x\frac{\widetilde b(s)}{\widetilde a(s)}\,ds\right],\quad C>0.}
$$
With $p=aw>0$, the result is the <Sturm-Liouville form> $L=-(p\,d/dx)'-wc$. The arbitrary positive multiplier $C$ rescales both sides of the <eigenvalue> problem and does not change its <eigenvalues>. The coefficient $\widetilde a'$ is already present in the <divergence> term of $\widetilde L$; it must not be counted a second time in the exponential.

= b
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=b}

Fix one <self-adjoint> choice of separated homogeneous <Dirichlet boundary conditions> or <Neumann boundary conditions> at each endpoint. The stated product condition then makes the boundary term $[awy y']_\alpha^\beta$ vanish. Multiplication of $Ly=\lambda wy$ by $y$ and <integration by parts> give
$$
\lambda\int_\alpha^\beta wy^2dx=\int_\alpha^\beta yLy\,dx=\int_\alpha^\beta[aw(y')^2-wcy^2]dx.
$$
Hence define the <quadratic functionals>
$$
\boxed{F[y]=\int_\alpha^\beta yLy\,dx=\int_\alpha^\beta[aw(y')^2-wcy^2]dx,\qquad G[y]=\int_\alpha^\beta wy^2dx,}
$$
and $\Lambda[y]=F[y]/G[y]$ for nonzero admissible $y$. The first expression for $F$ is linear in the operator $L$, and $G$ is linear in the weight $w$.

To prove the <minimum> statement, expand an admissible function in a complete weighted orthonormal system of <eigenfunctions> of the <Sturm-Liouville problem>, $y=\sum_n c_ny_n$, with ordered <eigenvalues> $\lambda_0\leq\lambda_1\leq\cdots$. Self-adjointness and <orthogonality of Sturm-Liouville eigenfunctions> give
$$
\Lambda[y]=\frac{\sum_n\lambda_n c_n^2}{\sum_n c_n^2}\geq\lambda_0.
$$
Equality is attained by a lowest <eigenfunction>, so
$$
\boxed{\lambda_{\min}=\min_{y\ne0}\Lambda[y].}
$$
This is the <Rayleigh-Ritz variational principle>. The endpoint product condition is shorthand for fixed choices of boundary type: allowing each trial function to choose different endpoint types would not define one fixed <self-adjoint> <eigenvalue> problem.

= c
{parent=10}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=c}

Move the differential terms to the left in the sign convention of part (a). Then $\widetilde a=1+x$, $\widetilde c=0$, and $\widetilde b=(3+2x)-\widetilde a'=2(1+x)$. The <integrating factor> is therefore $w=e^{2x}$, up to a positive constant. The <Sturm-Liouville problem> is
$$
\boxed{-\frac d{dx}\left[(1+x)e^{2x}y'\right]=\lambda e^{2x}y,\qquad y(0)=0,\quad y'(1)=0.}
$$
The trial $y=xe^{-x}$ is admissible because $y(0)=0$ and $y'=e^{-x}(1-x)$ vanishes at one. Its <Rayleigh quotient> has numerator
$$
F[y]=\int_0^1(1+x)(1-x)^2dx=\int_0^1(1-x-x^2+x^3)dx=\frac5{12}
$$
and denominator
$$
G[y]=\int_0^1x^2dx=\frac13.
$$
Consequently the <Rayleigh-Ritz variational principle> gives
$$
\boxed{\lambda_{\min}\leq\frac54.}
$$
The numerator is positive for every nonzero admissible function, so the lowest <eigenvalue> is positive. The trial is not an exact <eigenfunction>: substituting it would require $x^2+2x-1=\lambda x$ for every $x$. Thus the upper inequality is actually strict. This example underlies the <exponential-polynomial trial space for a mixed Sturm-Liouville problem>.

= d
{parent=10}
{scope}

= i
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=i}

For the added trial term $q_n=x^ne^{-Ax}$, the left <Dirichlet boundary condition> is automatic for a positive integer $n$. At the right endpoint,
$$
q_n'(1)=e^{-A}(n-A).
$$
To preserve $y'(1)=0$ for an arbitrary variational coefficient $C$, choose $\boxed{A=n}$. Then minimize the <Rayleigh quotient> of $xe^{-x}+Cx^ne^{-nx}$ over $C$.

Since $C=0$ remains available, this optimization cannot worsen the original upper bound. For $n=1$, the added function is a multiple of the first trial, so the quotient does not change: an independent choice $n\geq2$ is needed for extra freedom. More generally, several such functions form the <exponential-polynomial trial space for a mixed Sturm-Liouville problem>, and the <Rayleigh-Ritz method> reduces minimization to a small <generalized eigenvalue problem>.

= ii
{parent=d}
{scope}

= Solution
{parent=ii}

The added sine vanishes at zero. Its <derivative> at one is $B\cos B$, so nontrivial admissible choices are
$$
\boxed{B=\left(m+\frac12\right)\pi,\qquad m=0,1,2,\ldots.}
$$
Negative values give the same trial directions after changing the sign of $C$; $B=0$ adds nothing. For each choice, minimize the <Rayleigh quotient> of $xe^{-x}+C\sin(Bx)$ over $C$, or optimize over several coefficients simultaneously. The original trial is included, so the best bound is no larger than $5/4$. Enlarging the trial subspace successively is the <Rayleigh-Ritz method>; the boundary-compatible half-integer sines provide additional independent shapes.
